Split at `@`, build the masked local part from its length (first char + `*` × (len − 2) + last char, with the 1- and 2-character special cases), and lowercase the domain. O(n) time. A regex replacement or a character loop is equally fine.

def mask_email(email):
    local, domain = email.split("@")
    if len(local) >= 3:
        masked = local[0] + "*" * (len(local) - 2) + local[-1]
    elif len(local) == 2:
        masked = local[0] + "*"
    else:
        masked = local
    return masked + "@" + domain.lower()

Scan the lowercased title once, appending letters/digits and emitting a single `-` only when a separator run ends and both sides have content; or replace runs with a regex and strip dashes. O(n) time. A regex such as `re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", title.lower()).strip("-")` is an accepted alternative.

def slugify(title):
    parts, current = [], ""
    for ch in title.lower():
        if ch.isascii() and ch.isalnum():
            current += ch
        elif current:
            parts.append(current)
            current = ""
    if current:
        parts.append(current)
    return "-".join(parts) if parts else "untitled"

Cut the URL at the first `?` and then at `#`, split the rest on `&`, skip empty pieces, and split each piece at its first `=` into the map (later keys overwrite earlier ones). O(n) time. Manual index scanning instead of split is fine.

def parse_query(url):
    result = {}
    if "?" not in url:
        return result
    query = url.split("?", 1)[1].split("#", 1)[0]
    for piece in query.split("&"):
        if not piece:
            continue
        if "=" in piece:
            key, value = piece.split("=", 1)
        else:
            key, value = piece, ""
        result[key] = value
    return result

